Widen an invalidation to the bounds when the pixels come from a layer buffer, and the traces that
found it.

CMake typed text into its source field and the field showed only a caret. The text was in Qt, laid
out correctly: the caret sat exactly where four characters end, and it moved in proportion at four
and at twelve. Any full repaint, a compositor resize for instance, showed the text at once. Only
incremental repaints lost it.

CIDER_TRACE_LAYERBLIT prints two things: which layer buffer the software compositor is copying and
what rectangle it was asked for, and separately what every caller asks setNeedsDisplayInRect for,
with a backtrace when the rectangle has a zero origin. That named it in one run.

During typing, 112 of the invalidations were 0,0 949x21 and not one of them covered the line edit.
They were not Qt asking for anything. The backtrace put them in OUR OWN -[NSView addSubview:],
which marks the parent dirty with the frame of the view being added, and QMacStyle parents a cell
view while it draws. The size tracked the window, 949 wide at 1256 and 693 at 1000, so it looked
plausible enough to read as a real dirty region. The caret happened to fall inside that corner and
the text beside it did not, and that is the whole of the symptom.

The reason nothing better ever arrives: Qt flushes a whole frame by assigning it to layer.contents
and tells CoreAnimation which part changed. There is no CoreAnimation here, so every rectangle
reaching setNeedsDisplayInRect for such a view came from somewhere else and describes something
else. A layer buffer has no partial truth. Widening to the bounds composites the frame the toolkit
handed over, which is what a compositor would have done.

Views that draw themselves are untouched, having no layer contents.

MEASURED, ink in the field rectangle, thresholded: before 5, 11, 17 over three runs, the caret
alone; after 76, 93, 93 over three runs, the text and the caret. Resize still correct at 75, 92, 92.
Zero faults.

CMake is still NOT on the roster: typing a path pops the QCompleter and that still crashes in
QWindow::type on a bogus platform window pointer, which is task #227 and a different defect.

